WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.480
TOM SCOTT: The Youth Winter Olympic Games

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:05.280
have brought me out to Lillehammer, in Norway,
and to this:

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.259
the Hunderfossen Bobsleigh Track,

00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:12.709
the second largest freezer in Norway.

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:16.660
I know, you see "Norway" and "freezer",

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.940
and you think about the seed vault that's
up in the Arctic

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.140
which is technically bigger than this

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.770
but this track really is one giant freezer.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:29.240
This track stays frozen in temperatures up
to 20°C

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.930
so athletes can speed down here

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.070
and pull 4G on the final turn

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.860
even when there really shouldn't be any ice
for them to slide on.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.720
JAN ODDVAR TANGEN: We can keep it frozen

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:45.560
during the summer -- in a normal Norwegian
summer!

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.750
It's built with concrete. Inside the concrete,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.800
there are lots of layers with pipes.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.470
Inside the pipes, it's running with ammonia.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:59.070
TS: When a liquid evaporates, it pulls in
heat from the surrounding area.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.270
So there are 90 tonnes of ammonia circulating
in pipes

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.350
inside this freezer, underneath the track,

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.299
being repeatedly compressed and then evaporated

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:11.590
to move heat away. Ammonia, by the way,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.710
really dangerous if it's not handled properly.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:21.159
JOT: Because ammonia is the fastest working
fluid to drag away the warmth.

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:24.680
All the ammonia is going inside 65km of pipes

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.820
and it will stay inside the pipes in all conditions.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.460
If something happens, I will shut down the
spots

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.640
that accidentally have the leaks.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.820
It's lighter than air, so it will be going
upwards

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:40.950
so you won't be harmed!

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.659
TS: But the laws of thermodynamics always
hold true.

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:46.820
You can't create cold. You can only ever move
heat.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.299
And in the process of moving it, you generate
more heat.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:52.100
That's why the back of your fridge at home
is warm,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.520
and why air conditioning units need to vent
outside.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.970
And on a freezer this big? That's quite a
lot of heat to move.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.100
JOT: The first thing we're doing is cleaning
the track

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.320
with normal hoses with water, cleaning from
the summer.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.580
And then, start of the season, we're running
with luge the first week,

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.870
and then we start with the skeleton bob after
approximately 10 days

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.800
because then the ice thickness is thick enough

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.060
to cope with the bob and skeleton.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.849
TS: And with bobsleigh and luge runs being
measured

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:23.160
in hundredths of a second,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.150
keeping the ice consistently frozen

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.050
is important for what is called "competitive
parity":

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.599
basically, making sure everyone has a fair
shot.

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:34.550
A little bit more or less friction over a
day could change the result.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.450
And when there's this much riding on how fast
someone goes down a track?

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.080
Turns out having a big freezer is really important.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.650
There's a load more videos over on the Olympics
channel

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.830
and on my channel, go check 'em out, go subscribe,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.930
and thank you to the Youth Winter Olympic
Games

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.650
for bringing me out here to Lillehammer.

